I'm not quite sure what that tries to say
(could be just my bad English or 0:30am)
69b0d3dfe919a6b860a9fac82de52a7e: how did you come up with the Sec-WebSocket-KeyX thing?
especially the part which allows dividing by 0
er
ah it is explained
still, this all sounds strange
it's pretty crazy yeah
it was hard to come up with something that's hard to screw up
not sure what you don't understand about hte origin thing... can you elaborate?
not sure which part to explain :-)
it's just saying that if you have a simple script that just expects to talk to pages hosted from one page, it can just hard-code the Sec-WebSocket-Origin response field
but if it's a more complicated script that talks to many sites on the web, it'll need to look at the Origin field from the client first
and then only echo the one that the client sent
69b0d3dfe919a6b860a9fac82de52a7e: the multiple origins part
69b0d3dfe919a6b860a9fac82de52a7e: so client sends Origin: and server sends back Sec-WebSocket-Origin:
and in some cases Origin:
which is the same as what client sent to server?
the server always sends back Sec-WebSocket-Origin
and its value is either hard-coded, or derived from the client's Origin:
why is Sec-WebSocket-Origin needed?
to make sure you can't talk to a server who doesn't want to talk to you
(and so that the client is the one doing the check, not the server)
(since we don't trust the server to get things right)
well, if also server sends Origin
server never sends Origin
Origin: is the field that describes the client's origin
oh, ok
my bad
misread
thanks
np
still, the protocol is getting far from being trivial to implement
it's still pretty simple on the server. the client isn't trivial, certainly.
the latest changes added about 50% extra complexity to the server based on my attempts to implement a perl one
it was 99 lines, it's now about 150
69b0d3dfe919a6b860a9fac82de52a7e: &quot;; the wire protocol including error-handling and forward-compatible parsing rules&quot; doesn't seem to contain closing frame
looks
closing-frame is part of binary-frame in that version
ah, right
again, hard to read
I wonder how the protocol can be really used in isp webservers
the recent changes got rid of all the binary blobs and stuff that people complained about, so hopefully it's a little easier now
I doubt I can reserve any ports for my use
you mean web hosting providers?
yeah
dreamhost allow me to run arbitrary scripts that open any port above 1024
dunno about others
interesting
surprising
if you can run arbitrary code on unix, it's pretty hard to stop it, as i understand it
short of a firewall
dunno how common that is
69b0d3dfe919a6b860a9fac82de52a7e: still another thing, which was mentioned in hybi list
the need for bigint
do we really need to be able to handle huge binary frames?
you can just bail if you get a length that's too big
i don't see why people would send long lengths today
but when everyone is using 256 bit chips and petabit ethernet to the house, i don't see why we'd want to rev the entire protocol
well, that is something to mention in the spec, that bailing out is ok
it says &quot;WARNING: User agents may impose implementation-specific
limits on the lengths of invalid frames that they will skip. &quot;
maybe i should bring that higher up in the algorithm
bb21c722adad611b63dfb48ec99e8350: i tweaked the spec so the warnings are closer to where the bytes are read. should be on the site momentarily.

